  "Number": 8,
  "Question": "Let \\(a,\\) \\(a',\\) \\(b,\\) \\(b'\\) be real numbers with \\(a\\) and \\(a'\\) nonzero. The solution to \\(ax+b=0\\) is less than the solution to \\(a'x+b'=0\\) if and only if",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(a'b\\lt ab'\\)",
    "B": "\\(ab'\\lt a'b\\)",
    "C": "\\(ab\\lt a'b'\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\frac ba\\lt\\frac{b'}{a'}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\frac{b'}{a'}\\lt\\frac ba\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Since \\(a\\ne0\\) and \\(a'\\ne0\\), the equations have the unique solutions\n\\[\nx=-\\frac ba\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\nx=-\\frac{b'}{a'}.\n\\]\nThe first solution is smaller exactly when\n\\[\n-\\frac ba<-\\frac{b'}{a'}.\n\\]\nMultiplying both sides by \\(-1\\) reverses the inequality, giving\n\\[\n\\frac{b'}{a'}<\\frac ba.\n\\]\nEvery step is reversible, so this is an if-and-only-if condition. It works for either sign of \\(a\\) and \\(a'\\). The answer is choice E.",
